= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Schmidt decomposition> is $|\psi\rangle=\sum_js_j|a_j\rangle|b_j\rangle$. Its reduced states are $\rho_A=\sum_js_j^2|a_j\rangle\langle a_j|$ and $\rho_B=\sum_js_j^2|b_j\rangle\langle b_j|$. They have the same nonzero eigenvalues, so
$$
\boxed{S(\rho_A)=S(\rho_B)}.
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the pure state $\sigma_{RQE}$ induced by a purification and <Stinespring dilation>, the <coherent information> is
$$
\boxed{I_c(\Lambda,\rho)=S(\sigma_Q)-S(\sigma_E)
=-H(R|Q)_\sigma}.
$$

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose any pure input $\rho=|\phi\rangle\langle\phi|$. Its reference system may be one-dimensional, and the Stinespring output on $QE$ is pure. Its two reduced states have equal entropy by part a, hence
$$
\boxed{I_c(\Lambda,\rho)=0}.
$$

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Because $\sigma_{RQE}$ is pure, $S(RQ)=S(E)$ and $S(RE)=S(Q)$. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
I(R:Q)-I(R:E)
&=[S(R)+S(Q)-S(RQ)]\\
&\quad-[S(R)+S(E)-S(RE)]\\
&=2[S(Q)-S(E)].
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{I_c(\Lambda,\rho)=\frac12[I(R:Q)-I(R:E)]}.
$$

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For an <anti-degradable quantum channel>, a channel from $E$ simulates $Q$. The <data-processing inequality for quantum relative entropy> applied to <quantum mutual information> gives $I(R:Q)\leq I(R:E)$. Part d then implies $I_c(\Lambda,\rho)\leq0$ for every input. Part c supplies a pure input attaining zero, so
$$
\boxed{Q^{(1)}(\Lambda)=\max_\rho I_c(\Lambda,\rho)=0}.
$$

= f
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Assume an anti-degradable channel transmits perfectly with encoder $\mathcal E$ and decoder $\mathcal D$. Apply its Stinespring isometry to $\mathcal E(\rho)$, producing receiver system $Q$ and environment $E$. The receiver obtains $\rho$ by $\mathcal D$; anti-degradability lets the environment simulate the receiver output using $\mathcal N$, and then $\mathcal D\circ\mathcal N$ also produces $\rho$.

Apply these two local decoding channels simultaneously to $Q$ and $E$. Each marginal of the resulting bipartite state is the original pure state $\rho$. A bipartite state with a pure marginal is a product, so the joint output is $\rho\otimes\rho$. We have therefore constructed one quantum channel mapping every pure $\rho$ to $\rho\otimes\rho$, contradicting the <no-cloning theorem for two pure states>. Hence no anti-degradable channel can transmit arbitrary quantum information perfectly in one use.
